    hyunjun[smile]: Okay. I'm keeping that. I did it at full price.
* [quiet] Sit down on the stair next to him.
    @rel hyunjun closeness+1
    > The step is narrow. Your shoulders don't quite fit side by side, so his ends up a little in front of yours, like a door left ajar.
    hyunjun[smile]: Yes. Sit. We have time. We have more time than I know what to do with.
> For about four seconds he is completely at peace.
> Then you watch the hour and forty-seven minutes arrive in his head, all at once, like a train into a station.
hyunjun: Right. So. The roof, because the rain's stopped and you can see the bridge from the corner.
hyunjun: And the practice room. And the store again, the cat'll be up. And the other store. And the bus stop, we didn't finish the bus stop.
> He's patting his pockets. He comes up with the receipt from the convenience store. One item on it. Then a pen you've never seen before.
you: Where did you get a pen?
hyunjun: Front desk. Earlier. I was going to give it back. I'm still going to give it back.
> He flattens the receipt against the wall and starts writing on the blank side. Fast. The pen skips where the paper is thermal and slippery.
hyunjun: The lounge. The vending machine, to say hi. The laundromat — we should check on the dryer, it was worried. The loud lamp. The *second* loudest lamp, for comparison—
> The list goes down the receipt and turns the corner at the bottom. Eleven. Twelve. He squeezes fourteen in sideways, up the margin.
> Then his hand stops.
@expr hyunjun thinking
hyunjun: ...Oh.
hyunjun: I'm doing it again. It took me two minutes.
> He holds the receipt out at arm's length and looks at it the way you'd look at a photo of yourself from a bad year.
hyunjun: It gave me one good hour. One and a bit. And the first thing I do is try to fit nine nights into it.
hyunjun: Fourteen stops. We'd be running. I wouldn't see any of them. I'd be at every one going *okay, next*.
hyunjun[thinking]: That's not more night. That's the same night, faster.
> He isn't upset, exactly. He's doing what he does in corridors: walking round a thing until he can see the back of it.
hyunjun: Help me. You're the tiebreaker. You've been the tiebreaker since you were whatever-age-you-were on a train.
hyunjun[smile]: How do I make this a normal size?
* [sincere] "Which ones would you actually be sad to miss?"
    @rel hyunjun trust+1
    > He reads down the list. His pen hovers. It comes down on three of them, slowly, like he's surprised it's only three.
    hyunjun[thinking]: ...Three. The rest I wrote down because there was paper.
    hyunjun[smile]: That's a terrible reason to go somewhere. *Because there was paper.*
