> Four circles. He draws a fifth, at the bottom of the line. Bigger than the others, the way a terminus is on a real map.
> He holds the pen over it for a while. He doesn't write anything.
hyunjun[thinking]: The last one's blank.
hyunjun: I don't know what it is yet. Usually that's where I'd write six things, so it wasn't empty.
> He caps the pen, turns it round, and holds it out to you, the blunt end first.
hyunjun[smile]: You name it. Not now. When we get there. You've always been better at the last stop than I am.
* [sincere] Take the pen. "I'll know it when we're standing on it."
    @rel hyunjun trust+1
    hyunjun[smile]: Yeah. That's how you did Sindorim. You just stood up and knew.
    hyunjun: I hated that. I'm borrowing it now.
* [playful] Take the pen. "You're trusting me with the front desk's property."
    @rel hyunjun closeness+1
    hyunjun[laugh]: It's a shared crime now. If they ask, we were together the whole time. Which is true. That's the best kind of alibi.
* [romantic] Take the pen, and keep hold of the receipt's other corner a second longer. {if hyunjun.romance >= 2}
    @rel hyunjun romance+1
    > For a moment you're both holding the same thin strip of paper, one thumb each. It would tear if either of you pulled. Neither of you pulls.
    hyunjun[smile]: ...It's a good map. I can tell already. It has the right people on it.
@flag hyunjunx_map
> He folds the receipt once, along the line, and puts it in the chest pocket of the bomber. Then he takes it out to check it's still a map. It is.
hyunjun[smile]: An hour forty-seven, five stops. Twenty minutes each, near enough. That's generous.
hyunjun: We can walk. At an ordinary pace. Come on. Stop one. He's had that bag for over two hours, and he's fast.
