> The practice room smells of floor polish and old speakers. Somebody left the lights on the lowest setting.
> One wall is mirror, floor to ceiling. No tunnel behind it. No train attached. Just the room again, and the two of you in it.
@show hyunjun thinking at=c
> Hyunjun walks a lap of it before he says anything. You've stopped expecting otherwise.
hyunjun: So. The thing I can't do.
hyunjun: You know at the end of a song? The music stops, and you hold the last pose. Eight counts. Breathing hard. The camera stays on you.
hyunjun[smile]: People love that part. It's the part where you get to look at someone who just finished something.
you: And?
hyunjun: And I walk off on five.
hyunjun[smile]: Every time. I don't decide to. I hit the pose, it's good, one, two, three, four — and then I'm already *going* somewhere. My feet leave without me.
> He shows you. He snaps into a shape: one arm up, chin turned, weight on the back foot. It's good. Even at quarter effort, it's good.
> Then, right on time, his heel lifts, his shoulders turn, and he's walking toward the door with his arm still half in the air.
hyunjun: There. I didn't even feel that happen.
* [curious] "What's at five? What happens in your head?"
    @rel hyunjun trust+1
    hyunjun[thinking]: ...The next thing. Water. The next song. What I'm going to say to everyone after.
    hyunjun: Four is the last count where it's still *happening*. Five is when it's finished and I'm just standing in it.
* [playful] "Your feet have their own schedule. Songha would be proud."
    @rel hyunjun closeness+1
    hyunjun[smile]: My feet keep a better schedule than Songha does. My feet have never lost a block.
