> One stop past the river. A building you've had the key to for two weeks, on a street whose name you still have to check.
> You go up alone. You come down with a box.
> It's not heavy. The tape on it is three flats old: brown, then clear over the brown, then a strip of blue from somewhere you'd forgotten.
> You put it on the front step. Hyunjun stays on the pavement, exactly where he said he would, and opens the paper bag.
hyunjun[smile]: Scissors. Two thousand five hundred won. I said I'd hold the scissors. I didn't have any. It was bothering me.
> He hands them over, handles first, and then turns to look, with great interest, at a parked scooter.
you: You can look.
hyunjun: I said I wouldn't.
you: I'm asking you to. That's what I want the person by the door for.
> He turns round. He comes and sits on the step, on the other side of the box, and waits.
> The blue tape goes first. Then the clear. Then the brown.
> Inside, there is a shoebox, and inside the shoebox there are transit cards.
> A dozen of them. More. Every city you ever lived in, each in its own colours, held together with a hair elastic that's gone slack.
hyunjun[surprised]: You kept them all?
you: They've all got money on. A little. One ride, maybe two.
you: I never ran one down to zero. If I did, I was finished with the place.
> He picks up the top one and turns it over, carefully, the way he handled the glass card.
hyunjun[thinking]: One more stop. On every one of them.
hyunjun: You've been doing it too. The whole time. You just did it quietly, in a shoebox, and I did it out loud in restaurants.
> You hadn't known that was what the box was. Now it's open, it seems the only thing it could have been.
> He unclips the card from his own lanyard, the one with the fox charm, and holds it next to yours. Same city. Same green.
hyunjun[smile]: This one you run down. All the way. Then you top it up, and run it down again. That's what staying is. It's really boring. It's just topping up a card.
you: You make it sound easy.
hyunjun: It's not. I'll remind you. That's my job now.
@cam closeup hyunjun
hyunjun[thinking]: Can I say the real thing? I worked it out on the bridge.
hyunjun: I want to be your first number. The one you ring. Flat tyre, bad day, new ice cream, can't find the good exit at a station.
hyunjun[smile]: And you're mine. You already are. You were the vote all night. I'm just making it official.
hyunjun: It's not instead of anything. It's not the small version. It's the thing. I've got seven people I'd walk across the city for. Now I've got eight.
> You have had a phone for years. The line that says who to call has always been blank, because you were never anywhere long enough to fill it in.
> You fill it in on the step. He watches you type his name, and corrects the spelling of his own nickname for himself, which is *Inside Lane*.
you: Inside Lane?
hyunjun[smile]: I gave you the inside lane in the hallway at two a.m. You don't remember. It was a significant gesture.
> He stands up. Brushes off his knees.
hyunjun: Right. I'm going home. I can, because it's not the end of anything.
hyunjun[smile]: Saturday. I'm bringing all seven. We're doing the rest of your boxes. Songha's going to make a schedule for your kitchen, and you're going to let him.
you: Saturday.
hyunjun: Bring nothing. You live here.
> He goes. Down the street, past the scooter, toward the bridge. He doesn't ask for one more of anything.
> At the corner he turns round and walks four steps backwards, smiling, to check you're still on the step. You are.
